---
import BrandGuide from '../../components/BrandGuide.astro';

const fitSections = [
  {
    category: 'Jeans & denim',
    text: "Levi's sizes its jeans by actual waist and inseam inches, and modern stretch fits like the 511, 721, and 312 tend to run true to the labeled waist: measure your natural waist and you're most of the way there. The classics are the exception: rigid, non-stretch denim commonly fits tighter than the label suggests, and 501 Shrink-to-Fit is famous for tightening further after the first washes, so many shoppers go up in those styles.",
  },
  {
    category: 'Tops',
    text: "Levi's tees, western shirts, and flannels tend to follow a standard American cut, neither notably slim nor oversized. Many shoppers find graphic tees and batwing-logo styles fit true with a classic, slightly relaxed body, while heritage and vintage-inspired shirts can feel boxier through the chest. When in doubt, staying with your usual US letter size is the commonly reported safe bet.",
  },
  {
    category: 'Jackets & trucker',
    text: "The trucker jacket is deliberately cut short and trim in the body. That cropped silhouette is the design, not a sizing error. Many shoppers take their true size for the classic close fit, but if you plan to layer a hoodie or heavy flannel underneath, going up one is a common move. Sherpa-lined truckers tend to feel snugger than the unlined version in the same size because the lining takes up interior room.",
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Should I size up in Levi's?",
    a: "It depends on the fabric. Modern stretch fits tend to run true to the labeled waist, and some shoppers even find they relax slightly with wear, so between sizes going down is common there. Rigid, non-stretch denim has no give, so between sizes most shoppers go up. And for 501 Shrink-to-Fit, sizing up is standard advice because the raw denim shrinks after washing.",
  },
  {
    q: "Are Levi's 501s true to size?",
    a: "The regular pre-shrunk 501 tends to fit close to true, though its straight, non-stretch cut feels less forgiving than stretch styles. The 501 Shrink-to-Fit version is a different story: it's sold unshrunk and is commonly reported to lose around an inch to an inch and a half in the waist and more in length after washing, so buyers traditionally size up to land at their target fit.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do Levi's stretch out over time?",
    a: "Stretch-blend styles tend to relax modestly through the waist and thigh with wear, which is why many shoppers prefer them snug out of the box. Rigid 100% cotton denim also molds and loosens a little where your body flexes, but far less predictably; it won't rescue a pair that's genuinely too tight in the waist.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does W32 L32 mean on Levi's jeans?",
    a: "W is the waist and L is the inseam (inside leg), both in inches, so a 32×32 is intended for roughly a 32-inch waist with a 32-inch leg length. Because many brands vanity-size their labels, your Levi's W number may be larger than the size you wear elsewhere; measuring a well-fitting pair of jeans you already own is the most reliable starting point.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do Levi's women's sizes work?",
    a: "Most women's Levi's are sold by waist size (W25 through W34 and beyond) alongside a US size equivalent, with separate inseam options on many fits. High-rise stretch styles like the 721 tend to run true to the waist number, and curvy-cut fits are designed to add hip room without changing the waist, which is helpful if you usually size up just to clear your hips.",
  },
];

const related = [
  { href: '/size-guides/jeans-inseam-length-guide/', label: 'Jeans inseam & length guide' },
  { href: '/size-guides/gap-size-chart-fit-guide/', label: 'Gap size chart & fit guide' },
  { href: '/blog/vanity-sizing-explained/', label: 'Vanity sizing explained' },
];
---

<BrandGuide
  brandKey="levis"
  brand="Levi's"
  title="Levi's Size Chart & Fit Guide: Does It Run Small?"
  description="Levi's stretch fits tend to run true to the labeled waist; rigid and Shrink-to-Fit styles run tight, so size up. Fit notes by style plus sizing FAQ."
  fitSections={fitSections}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={related}
>
  <Fragment slot="verdict">
    <strong>Levi's tends to run true to size in modern stretch fits</strong> because the brand
    labels by real waist inches rather than vanity sizes. The catch is the classics: rigid denim
    and 501 Shrink-to-Fit commonly fit tight and tighten further after washing, so many shoppers
    size up in those — and trust the tape measure, not the number they wear at other brands.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="tips">
    <li><strong>Measure first:</strong> Levi's W number is meant to match your actual waist in inches, which is often larger than the vanity-sized label you wear elsewhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Match strategy to fabric:</strong> between sizes, many shoppers go down in stretch fits (they tend to relax with wear) and up in rigid, non-stretch denim.</li>
    <li><strong>Shrink-to-Fit needs headroom:</strong> the unshrunk 501 is commonly bought a size up in the waist and longer in the leg to allow for wash shrinkage.</li>
    <li><strong>Truckers run short on purpose:</strong> take your true size for the classic cropped look, or go up one if you'll layer sweaters underneath.</li>
  </Fragment>
</BrandGuide>
